            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The primary meaning is the unregenerate, sinful human nature and a hardened heart alienated from God. Alternatives include ethnic boundary markers, ritual impurity, and functional impediments or obstructions (such as uncircumcised lips, ears, or fruit trees).\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Obstructing flesh that covers a covenant member and prevents its proper work, whether it be humility (heart), hearing (ears), speech (lips), or fruitfulness (trees).\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"The unregenerate human nature, sinful flesh, or a hardened, stubborn heart.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Obstructing flesh that covers a covenant member and prevents its proper work.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_BROADENS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"SAME_CASES\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book elevates the concept of a functional 'obstruction' into the unified primary definition for all usages. Whereas the consensus treats the spiritualized 'sinful nature' as the primary meaning and categorizes lips, ears, and trees as separate minority metaphors, the book views them all as equal expressions of obstructing flesh.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. The book accommodates the consensus view of the hardened heart (blocking humility) but reframes it as one instance of a broader unifying principle that seamlessly includes hearing, speech, and agricultural fruitfulness.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book synthesizes the consensus's primary meaning and its various alternatives into a single, broader functional category. The referent cases remain identical, but the defining core principle is abstracted to cover them all uniformly.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The theological weight of 'uncircumcision' in the New Testament (e.g., total alienation from God, being dead in sin) may be underrepresented by reducing it to a functional 'obstruction'